> Not sure I understand it correctly, I think plugins should run with initialized data for each schedule cycle, and should not keep data across each cycle, then there will be no problem?
>
> I am afraid I don't fully understand the problem. Are you saying that some plugins may maintain an internal state and the state needs to be cleaned up? Can we design the plugins to be stateless?

@hex108 @liu-cong We **could** design the plugins to be stateless, but I don't think it should be the only option. And in fact, we have an [example](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/bc1d8c6d61078269540f807248a455cba09e672c/pkg/scheduler/framework/plugins/examples/stateful/stateful.go#L32-L78) of a stateful plugin, and as bobby mentioned before - score plugin could keep whatever internal state as it wants to calculate the priority.

Besides, there are two cycles in the scheduling framework, in a binding cycle:

+ if it is aborted, it will trigger the un-reserve phase.
+ if it is successful, it will trigger the post-bind phase.

However, we don't have such a thing in the scheduling cycle. If the scheduling cycle is accidentally aborted, we can't get a callback in the plugin.

Use plugin context to pass the data through extension point could be an alternative (e.g. alloc the data structure in post-filter and store it in the plugin context). As data stores in the `PluginContext` can be read, altered, or deleted by another plugin, and there is a possibility that different plugin could use the same key, if the data is only used inside the plugin, an internal state is preferred here IMO.

> // PluginContext provides a mechanism for plugins to store and retrieve arbitrary data.
// ContextData stored by one plugin can be read, altered, or deleted by another plugin.
// PluginContext does not provide any data protection, as all plugins are assumed to be
// trusted.
